1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,130
Each map holds five hundred thirty-seven bomb hits.

2
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:06,410
One is pure chance; the other follows a rule.

3
00:00:06,910 --> 00:00:08,060
Which is chance?

4
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,140
Both are simulated.

5
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:12,820
The question isn't.

6
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:21,380
In nineteen forty-four, Londoners claimed flying bombs fell in clusters, and precise aim would put factories at risk.

7
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:24,163
Build the detector your eye is running.

8
00:00:24,463 --> 00:00:31,893
Cut each map into five hundred seventy-six squares, and flag any square with three or more hits, like this one.

9
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,390
Map A lights up with thirty-nine targets, map B with none.

10
00:00:36,890 --> 00:00:38,200
So A looks aimed.

11
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:39,543
Backwards.

12
00:00:40,443 --> 00:00:46,213
I dropped every hit on map A at random, so all thirty-nine targets are false alarms.

13
00:00:46,513 --> 00:00:50,683
Map B's rule: no two hits closer than four fifths of a square.

14
00:00:50,983 --> 00:00:53,303
Your eye expects chance to look that even.

15
00:00:53,766 --> 00:00:54,936
Take one square.

16
00:00:55,336 --> 00:00:59,106
Each bomb lands in it with chance one in five seventy-six.

17
00:00:59,606 --> 00:01:03,226
To stay empty, all five hundred thirty-seven must miss.

18
00:01:03,666 --> 00:01:05,586
Guess how many squares stay empty.

19
00:01:07,533 --> 00:01:09,293
About two hundred twenty-seven.

20
00:01:09,593 --> 00:01:11,583
Map A has two hundred twenty-two.

21
00:01:12,733 --> 00:01:20,133
Those empty squares force the clumps: the other three hundred fifty-four share all the hits, so many must double up.

22
00:01:20,433 --> 00:01:23,593
To judge London, chance must predict every bar.

23
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:26,243
Call the average hits per square lambda.

24
00:01:26,743 --> 00:01:33,213
One minus a small slice, raised to a big power, is almost exactly e to the minus lambda.

25
00:01:34,166 --> 00:01:43,136
For two hits in this square, pick two bombs: five thirty-seven times five thirty-six ways, over two, since order doesn't matter.

26
00:01:43,536 --> 00:01:50,196
Each lands with chance one in five seventy-six, and the rest miss: e to the minus lambda again.

27
00:01:50,633 --> 00:01:53,513
Five thirty-six is almost five thirty-seven.

28
00:01:53,913 --> 00:01:57,973
For three hits, each two becomes a three; for any k, a k.

29
00:01:58,473 --> 00:02:02,933
The powers merge into lambda to the k: the Poisson distribution.

30
00:02:03,933 --> 00:02:08,183
Times five seventy-six squares, it predicts these purple bars.

31
00:02:08,633 --> 00:02:11,693
The honest detector compares whole histograms.

32
00:02:12,293 --> 00:02:15,483
Thirty-nine targets, and chance predicts thirty-nine.

33
00:02:15,883 --> 00:02:23,823
The chi-squared misfit squares each bar's gap and divides by chance's bar, so a miss on a short bar counts more.

34
00:02:24,123 --> 00:02:25,853
Map A scores two point oh.

35
00:02:26,353 --> 00:02:29,533
Three in four simulated chance maps score worse.

36
00:02:29,833 --> 00:02:32,363
That share, p, is point seven four.

37
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:40,710
Map B: half the expected empty squares, a tower of single hits, misfit two hundred seventy-two.

38
00:02:41,210 --> 00:02:44,830
A pattern this even is the fingerprint of a spacing rule.

39
00:02:45,266 --> 00:02:50,336
Aimed bombs would miss the other way: extra empty squares, extra pile-ups.

40
00:02:50,636 --> 00:03:01,136
In nineteen forty-six, R D Clarke published the same test on London's real hits: five hundred thirty-seven, in five hundred seventy-six squares.

41
00:03:01,600 --> 00:03:07,560
Chance predicted two hundred twenty-seven empty squares; Clarke counted two hundred twenty-nine.

42
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:10,130
Overall, p equals point eight eight.

43
00:03:11,566 --> 00:03:19,046
The bombs were aimed at central London but tended to fall short, so farther north, hits were about half as dense.

44
00:03:19,346 --> 00:03:23,656
The test needs one lambda: Clarke chose an area where it held steady.

45
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:25,210
So look again.

46
00:03:26,210 --> 00:03:29,110
Map A's clumps are what no aim at all looks like.

47
00:03:29,410 --> 00:03:32,090
If you chose map B, you chose the rule.

